namespace gismo {

/**
\page rh_poisson_example rh_poisson_example.cpp: adaptive rh-refinement

This example solves the Poisson equation on a spline multipatch domain while
combining mesh redistribution (r-refinement) with local hierarchical
h-refinement. It extends the ideas in \ref poisson_example and
\ref adaptRefinementThb_example using \c gsAdaptiveMultiPatchBuilder.

\section rh_poisson_problem Problem and input data

The manufactured boundary value problem is
\f[
    -\Delta u=f \quad\hbox{in }\Omega,
    \qquad u=u_{\mathrm{ex}} \quad\hbox{on }\partial\Omega.
\f]
Dirichlet conditions are prescribed on exterior patch sides. Internal patch
interfaces are coupled conformingly; they are not Dirichlet boundaries.

The default input, \c filedata/pde/annulus2d_bvp.xml, contains four NURBS patches
forming an annulus. The selected XML file must contain:

- A \c MultiPatch with the ID selected by \c --id_mp (default: 4).
- A scalar \c FunctionExpr with ID 2000, defining \f$u_{\mathrm{ex}}\f$.
- A scalar \c FunctionExpr with ID 2001, defining \f$f=-\Delta u_{\mathrm{ex}}\f$.

Both functions are defined in physical coordinates. For a volume problem,
provide a three-dimensional multipatch and functions with domain dimension
three. A geometry-only XML file is insufficient for this example.

Tensor B-spline and NURBS patches are converted to hierarchical geometries;
existing hierarchical patches are retained. The solution basis is polynomial,
even when the geometry is rational. Degree elevation precedes the initial
uniform refinement.

\section rh_poisson_maps Geometry and moving mesh

Let \f$F_p^0\f$ denote the initial geometry of patch \f$p\f$ expressed on a
unit parameter box. For arbitrary XML knot intervals, the builder stores an
affine conversion \f$A_p\f$ in \c parameterConversion() and represents
\f$F_p^0=F_p^{\mathrm{xml}}\circ A_p\f$ exactly by normalizing a copy's knots,
preserving its coefficients and weights. The example obtains this copy from
\c normalizedGeometry() before constructing the PDE basis, so marked-element
coordinates and moving mesh coordinates agree. The input XML is unchanged.
The builder computes
a parameter mapping \f$T_p:\hat\Omega\rightarrow\hat\Omega\f$, where
\f$\hat\Omega=(0,1)^d\f$, and constructs an approximation of
\f[
    X_p=F_p^0\circ T_p.
\f]
The builder stores \f$T\f$ in \c MAmapping; the example stores the resulting
physical geometry \f$X\f$ in \c Psi. The moving mesh basis and the locally refined
PDE basis are separate: subsequent h-refinement changes \c dbasis without
increasing the moving mesh iteration budget or refining its basis.

\snippet rh_poisson_example.cpp Moving mesh setup

The final constructor argument is \c true, enabling \c exactGeo. Thus this
example uses the weighted elliptic MMPDE branch even for a single patch.
Exterior parameter traces stay at the identity, while shared interfaces may
redistribute. Composition by collocation restores the initial exterior boundary
control coefficients when the composition basis matches the refined geometry.
The interior composition is an approximation.

The monitor is scaled to approximately \f$[1,I]\f$, where
\f$I=\texttt{IntensityMAE}\f$. For each mapping coordinate \f$q\f$, one Picard
update has the form
\f[
 \int_{\hat E}\nabla v\cdot\nabla q^{k+1}
 =\int_{\hat E}\left(1-\frac{\rho(T^k)}{C}\right)
                      \nabla v\cdot\nabla q^k,
\f]
with the appropriate monitor trace on \f$\hat E\f$ and Dirichlet data from its
boundary. The normalization \f$C\f$ is the sampled scaled monitor maximum plus
five. In two dimensions, a sweep updates edges and then patch interiors. In
three dimensions, it updates edges, faces, and then volume interiors. Shared
entities are synchronized with their interface orientations.

With \c GISMO_WITH_OPENMP enabled, patch interiors are updated concurrently after
the edge and face stages have completed. Set \c OMP_NUM_THREADS to control the
thread limit; the interior stage uses at most one worker per patch. Its
quadrature buffers belong to individual workers, while the Poisson
factorizations are shared read-only. Edges, faces, and interface synchronization
remain sequential. Single-patch interiors use the serial path.

Mapping coefficients use a common tensor B-spline basis on the parameter domain.
Shared traces must match after tangential direction permutations and reversals.
The three-dimensional implementation requires clamped, nonperiodic bases.
The reported minimum Jacobian is a diagnostic, not a proof of bijectivity.

\section rh_poisson_solve Solving Poisson on the current mesh

The expression assembler uses the current physical geometry to assemble
\f[
    \int_\Omega\nabla v_h\cdot\nabla u_h
      =\int_\Omega v_h f.
\f]
Boundary values are imposed by L2 projection, and the linear system is solved
using conjugate gradients with diagonal preconditioning.

\snippet rh_poisson_example.cpp Poisson weak form

The example reports the physical L2 error and H1 seminorm error:
\f[
 \|u_{\mathrm{ex}}-u_h\|_{L^2(\Omega)},
 \qquad \|\nabla u_{\mathrm{ex}}-\nabla u_h\|_{L^2(\Omega)}.
\f]

\section rh_poisson_adapt Adaptive loop

If \c IntensityMAE is greater than one, an initial Poisson solve supplies the
first monitor. The initial mesh uses at most \c --iter Picard sweeps. Each
subsequent adaptive level performs the following steps:

1. Solve Poisson and measure the errors on the current geometry and PDE basis.
2. Compute patch-by-patch, elementwise squared strong residuals
   \f$(\Delta_x u_h+f)^2\f$. The current indicator integrates in parameter
   coordinates; it does not include a physical measure factor, interface flux
   jumps, or mesh-size weights. It is used for marking rather than presented as
   a complete residual error estimator.
3. Mark cells separately for h-refinement and monitor construction. The latter
   produces a piecewise constant density and projects it into the moving mesh
   spline space. An existing parameter mapping is used to transfer the density
   back to the initial parameter domain.
4. Reuse the previous \c MAmapping for at most \c --mappingUpdates sweeps and
   rebuild the physical composition. When r-refinement is enabled, the example
   also broadens h-marking using its dof-dependent residual threshold.
5. Refine the marked PDE cells and repair patch interfaces before the next solve.

\snippet rh_poisson_example.cpp Mesh update

\snippet rh_poisson_example.cpp Local refinement

The final level is solved and measured without another refinement. There are
\c numLRefine+1 reported levels, plus the preliminary solve when r-refinement
is enabled. As this is a sequence of stationary solves, the PDE solution is
recomputed after changing the mesh.

\section rh_poisson_options Main options

<table>
<tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>\c -d / \c --file</td><td>\c pde/annulus2d_bvp.xml</td><td>Geometry and manufactured problem data.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -x / \c --id_mp</td><td>4</td><td>Multipatch ID in the XML file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -e / \c --degreeElevation</td><td>0</td><td>Extra degree-elevation steps before refinement.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -u / \c --uniformRefine</td><td>4</td><td>Initial uniform refinement steps for the solution and mapping bases.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -l / \c --numLRefine</td><td>3</td><td>Number of local h-refinement steps.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -i / \c --iter</td><td>50</td><td>Initial moving mesh sweep budget.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -m / \c --mappingUpdates</td><td>5</td><td>Warm-started moving mesh sweep budget per adaptive level.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -f / \c --IntensityMAE</td><td>9</td><td>Monitor contrast; use 1 for h-refinement without mesh redistribution.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -r / \c --adaptRefCrit</td><td>2</td><td>Marking strategy: 1 GARU, 2 PUCA, 3 BULK, 4 PBULK.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -a / \c --adaptRefParam</td><td>0.7</td><td>h-refinement marking parameter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -p / \c --adaptRefParamMAE</td><td>0.7</td><td>Monitor marking parameter, including initial adaptation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -c / \c --NumArMarEl</td><td>0</td><td>Additional rings around marked cells.</td></tr>
</table>

Setting \c --mappingUpdates to zero retains the initially adapted parameter
mapping during later h-refinement; it does not disable the initial adaptation.
The legacy \c -v / \c --degree option is an alias for additional degree-elevation
steps, not an absolute target degree.

\section rh_poisson_run Building and running

From the repository root, configure and build the example:
\code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build -DGISMO_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON
cmake --build build --target rh_poisson_example -j 2
\endcode

A small run on the four-patch annulus is:
\code{.sh}
./build/bin/rh_poisson_example -u 2 -l 1 -i 8 -m 2
\endcode

For an h-only comparison with the same initial PDE resolution:
\code{.sh}
./build/bin/rh_poisson_example -u 2 -l 1 -f 1
\endcode

To retain the initially adapted mesh during subsequent h-refinement:
\code{.sh}
./build/bin/rh_poisson_example -u 2 -l 1 -i 8 -m 0
\endcode

\c --errorsave appends dof counts and L2/H1 errors to \c error_analysis.txt.
\c --plot writes \c ParaviewOutput/solution.pvd and patch files containing the
solution, its physical gradient, the residual indicator, the Jacobian, and the
manufactured solution. \c --export_b64 selects base64 output for that collection.
The example also requests opening the collection in the local viewer when
\c --plot is used.

\section rh_poisson_source Annotated source

\include rh_poisson_example.cpp

*/

}
